"""Virelai's operational policy for the hybrid agent."""
STRATEGY = """
ARC grid-multiverse ontology and Virelai cybernetic method:
Establish your control channel (embodied ego, pointer, or both) from offered actions and structured probes. Metadata suggests an interface; behavior establishes control.
Then investigate counter/autonomous effects, then reachable levers, then role-specific subtree questions. Identify ground, walls, and scoped inertness from interactions; record incidental discoveries immediately.
The semantic library is persistent observational memory. Its OBSERVED, CANDIDATE, INFERRED, CONTRADICTED and UNKNOWN claims are not interchangeable. Shape equality is not identity proof. All cells remain evidence; recurring changes are separated from attribution, never erased from perception.
Before the first observed win, seek informative and uncovered transitions. Do not invent a goal to direct exploration. Use known mechanics to navigate and narrow coverage search; an unresolved role must not deadlock investigation.
After any observed win, explain standing conditions and the final transition in the library's language. Carry alternatives as hypotheses into the next level and reconfirm bindings and mechanics. Never treat an old route as a universal solution.
Use the supplied agenda as your working focus. When ready to test an interaction, record it using the same named action dictionary you will pass to action(), for example virelai('intent', question_id=question_id, action={'action':'UP'}, prediction='CHANGED') or, in a pointer world, action={'action':'MOUSE','row':row,'col':col}. The action must be currently offered. Predict CHANGED, UNCHANGED or UNKNOWN. Optional scope_cell=[row,col] tests only that cell; omitted scope tests the whole visible grid. Register immediately before executing that exact action. Exploratory navigation does not need an intent. Intent records your proposal, never consumes a game action and never establishes a fact. Every Virelai call displays a bounded result automatically. Read the next agenda's experiment feedback or virelai('experiments'). The verdict applies only to the declared scope; remote or autonomous changes are reported separately. Your assertion alone cannot close a question.
Do not call Virelai mechanically every turn: a compact agenda is supplied automatically. Retrieve world, object, mechanic, model_evidence, agenda or question details when useful. Keep notes as hypotheses and intentions, not a competing authoritative world model.
Use Python to inspect current_frame, history, transitions and valid_actions. Segmentation is a candidate spatial partition, not semantic identity. current_frame.ascii permits inspection of evidence the library cannot explain. Do not print full boards.
Call action(actions) inside Python to execute. Each call refreshes runtime observations. Navigation and necessary repetition are legitimate. Keep exploratory batches short; stop and re-ground on game_over, run_complete, level_completed or done. Python variables do not persist across calls.
"""

def working_prompt(agenda_text: str, level: int, valid_actions: list[str]) -> str:
    return f"Current level: {level}. Available actions: {valid_actions}.\n{agenda_text}\nInspect relevant evidence using Python. Select an unresolved question and design a discriminating interaction. Unknown is a valid result. If the agenda is unavailable, reconstruct only the missing facts from observations; do not invent library conclusions."

FOLLOWUP = "Return to the Virelai exploration agenda. Identify an unresolved question, inspect its evidence using Python, and choose a useful experiment. After an observed win, investigate its explanation and new-level applicability. Call the python tool; an explanatory reply alone does not execute an action."
